  "image_name": "00ce2fd77ad6281ed72c3a639e878c93.jpg",
  "prompt_text": "The figure summarizes inflammatory pathways inside a cell and highlights drugs that block key steps. At the top, reactive oxygen species (ROS) are generated by xanthine oxidase and NADPH oxidase; these sources are shown as targets of allopurinol and NOX inhibitors. Microtubules are also emphasized, with colchicine depicted as disrupting their role. On the left, cholesterol crystals appear to trigger assembly of the NALP3 inflammasome, which then activates caspase-1 and converts pro-IL-1β into IL-1β.\n\nIn the lower part of the cell, IL-1β and TNF-α signal through their receptors, feeding into intracellular pathways involving NF-κB and p38, which then influence the nucleus and inflammatory gene expression. Dilmapimod is shown inhibiting p38. Outside the cell membrane, biologic therapies block major cytokines: canakinumab targets IL-1β, while adalimumab, infliximab, and etanercept block TNF signaling.\n\nOverall, the image presents a network linking crystal-induced inflammasome activation, oxidative stress, cytokine release, and downstream transcriptional signaling, while comparing several anti-inflammatory treatments by the points in the pathway where they act.",
